{
  "id": 241942178,
  "iid": 11623,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "DHCP: Add preference for seconds elapsed field endianness",
  "description": "This change makes it possible to better troubleshoot DHCP cases where there are DHCP clients populating the `secs` field as little-endian and keep sending DHCP messages (for whatever reason), i.e. the `secs` value keeps increasing and the current field encoding autodetection changes behavior after 255 seconds.\n\nThis also helps in situations where a correctly-filled (big-endian) `secs` field with a large value but with lower 8 bits as zero is erroneusly interpreted as little-endian by the current dissector logic.\n\nThe current autodetect model is still the default after this change.\n\nSample of a misbehaving little-endian DHCP client case: [le-case-1_anon_anon.pcapng](/uploads/9a91bb52fa4e8039d752d61b30582956/le-case-1_anon_anon.pcapng)\n- With Wireshark 4.0.7 `dhcp.secs` values are: 19593, 37513, 55433, 7818, ...\n- With this patch and \"Little Endian\" pref: 35148, 35218, 35288, 35358, ... (= helps following the progress or lack thereof)",
  "state": "merged",
  "created_at": "2023-08-08T18:32:07.808Z",
  "updated_at": "2023-10-01T10:52:00.822Z",
